[1/6] choose backends
  note: toker runs as a socket-activated systemd user service, so it sees only the user manager's environment, not your shell's. Put OPENROUTER_API_KEY in ~/.config/environment.d/*.conf (read at login), or run `systemctl --user set-environment OPENROUTER_API_KEY=…`; then restart toker.service for a running service to pick it up

[2/6] write toker.toml
wrote [ROOT]/.config/toker/toker.toml
  port [PORT], awake on, backends anthropic_sub, openrouter
  anthropic → anthropic_sub; openai_chat → openrouter
  openrouter key: env OPENROUTER_API_KEY (systemd user environment) when set, else the frontend's own key

[3/6] install + start the units
installed toker.socket ([ROOT]/.config/systemd/user/toker.socket)
installed toker.service ([ROOT]/.config/systemd/user/toker.service)
systemctl --user daemon-reload — ok
systemctl --user enable --now toker.socket — ok

[4/6] verify the service answers
the /f/claude prefix → 200 (toker's own status)
POST /f/claude/v1/messages → 401 (the upstream's verdict)
POST /v1/messages → 401 (the upstream's verdict)
POST /v1/chat/completions → 401 (the upstream's verdict)

